# MarkdownLLM in Codex Cloud

Generated setup for {{repository}}. Commit these files to the primary repository;
the default branch must carry them because Codex builds its cache there first.

Configure the GitHub-backed environment once:

| Setting | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Workspace directory (base, excluding repository name) | `{{workspace_directory}}` |
| Setup mode | {{setup_mode}} |
| Setup command | `{{setup_command}}` |
| Maintenance command | `{{maintenance_command}}` |
| Agent internet | {{agent_internet}} |

Setup always has network access. No PAT is required to download the public
framework. Codex owns the primary checkout; Git access to other private
repositories must be established and verified separately.
`environment.json` is strict JSON, so the bootstrap needs only Python's standard
library before it creates the framework virtual environment with PyYAML 6.0.3.

The bootstrap preserves the primary checkout's selected branch/commit, index
and files. Each additional domain is an independent checkout under `domains/`;
ordinary code repositories live under `repositories/` and keep their own
build hooks. The shared Git floor is installed only in domains. The primary
clone gets `mdllm.publication=pr` and `mdllm.sync=observe`: the host's PR
flow publishes its result, while estate sync observes without moving its HEAD.
Additional repositories follow the explicit per-repository policies in
`environment.json`; neither setup nor maintenance commits or pushes.

The manifest names the exact branch or commit for each additional repository.
`access: read` is an operating intention, not a filesystem sandbox. Use a
read-scoped GitHub credential for real read-only access. `publication: manual`
disables automatic sends; `declared` is only valid for a writable domain and
still requires literal `git.autopush: true` in that domain's AGENTS.md. Codex's
primary PR is not a publication receipt for secondary repositories. Review
`cloud status` and each repository's remote after committing.

For `credential_env`, provide a variable **name** in the manifest and its
value in the cloud environment's persistent environment variables for every
phase that needs Git. A setup-only secret disappears before the working agent;
it cannot support agent-time fetch or publication. The bootstrap uses an exact
GitHub repository credential helper and never writes the token value to Git
config or the manifest. Leave `credential_env: null` only when native Git
authentication has been proved in the relevant phase. For a private domain's
ignored `.boundary-terms`, set `boundary_terms_env` to a persistent variable
name. The file is created with private permissions; inspect its cache handling
with your own data policy. Without it, no disclosure coverage is claimed.

Fresh setup and cached maintenance check the same full framework commit. A
changed pin requires Reset cache. Changing repository files alone does not
invalidate Codex's cache. Commit the revised files and reset it deliberately.

Setup logs are not agent context. At the start of a working task, run the
framework runtime's `cloud probe <primary>`, `cloud start <primary>
--repository <domain-name>` and `cloud status <primary>` from this checkout.
`cloud start` performs the bounded estate sync before emitting the selected
domain's complete Tier-0 contract; read it, then load that domain's routed
skills. It cannot establish model receipt during setup. The first real commit
still enforces the session gate. Existing `.codex/hooks.json` is inspected
separately with `mdllm doctor . --harness codex`.

The skill in `.agents/skills/configure-codex-cloud/` guides conversational
configuration through the available browser UI. It does not call an undocumented
environment API. Confirm actual setup execution and agent contract delivery in
a real cloud task before claiming this product surface is verified.

Official behaviour: https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/environments/cloud-environment
